1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,770

2
00:00:06,770 --> 00:00:07,440
OK, great.

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,370
So the first sort of paper that
I'm going to be talking about

4
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:13,490
is called emergent abilities
of large language models.

5
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:16,160
And this paper was
especially cool, I think,

6
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,130
because we got people
from Google and DeepMind,

7
00:00:19,130 --> 00:00:21,260
and also Stanford
you might recognize

8
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,380
Percy, or Tatsuya, or Rishi.

9
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:26,300
I mean, we got people
to agree on what's

10
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,710
a nice framework of looking
at why we want to scale

11
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:33,540
and emerging abilities.

12
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:38,810
So one of the things that we've
seen throughout language models

13
00:00:38,810 --> 00:00:41,090
is that you get these
predictable gains

14
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:42,380
as a result of scaling.

15
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:46,820
So here's the canonical
Kaplan et al paper,

16
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:49,400
where you can see
that, if you scale up

17
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,650
the size of the language
model, measured either

18
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:55,940
in compute, in data set
size, or in parameters.

19
00:00:55,940 --> 00:01:00,140
You see that the loss
on the test set actually

20
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,098
goes down predictably.

21
00:01:02,098 --> 00:01:03,140
With your screen sharing.

22
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:05,620
So people in Zoom, I don't
think, can see the slides.

23
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:06,350
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

24
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:06,850
Sorry.

25
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:09,830

26
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:11,840
OK.

27
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,740
I guess I'll say this
for the third time.

28
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:17,600
As we've seen in
language models,

29
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,150
if you scale up the
size of language model,

30
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:23,270
measured either in
compute, in data set size,

31
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:24,950
or a number of
parameters, you can

32
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:28,220
see that there's a sort of
this predictable improvement

33
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:28,955
in the test loss.

34
00:01:28,955 --> 00:01:32,380

35
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:34,380
Now, what I'm going to
be talking about in terms

36
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:38,430
of emergence, is something
that's actually unpredictable,

37
00:01:38,430 --> 00:01:40,530
if you only look at
smaller language models.

38
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:44,580
So one way that emergence has
been described in the broader

39
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:46,530
science literature
is, it's basically

40
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:49,470
seen as a qualitative
change that arises

41
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,510
from quantitative changes.

42
00:01:51,510 --> 00:01:54,240
It sort of started with
this article in science

43
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:56,550
by a Nobel Prize
winning physicist

44
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,300
called "More is Different."

45
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:03,440
And I really like this post
from Jacob Steinhardt that

46
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,420
sort of describes emergence.

47
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:08,639
He gives a couple of
good examples here.

48
00:02:08,639 --> 00:02:10,610
For example, he
says, with uranium.

49
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:13,430
With a bit of uranium,
nothing special happens.

50
00:02:13,430 --> 00:02:16,020
With the large amount of
uranium packed densely enough,

51
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:18,470
you get a nuclear reaction.

52
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:20,270
And then also, with
DNA, for example.

53
00:02:20,270 --> 00:02:22,760
Given only small
molecules such as calcium,

54
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:25,130
you can't meaningfully
encode useful information.

55
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:28,190
But given larger models such as
DNA, you can encode a genome.

56
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:31,000

57
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,430
So for this particular
work, we use this definition

58
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:38,700
of emergent abilities of large
language models in particular.

59
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:42,150
So we say that
ability is emergent

60
00:02:42,150 --> 00:02:44,430
if it is not present
in smaller models,

61
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:47,850
but it is present
in larger models.

62
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:49,800
And the sort of
natural question here

63
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:53,190
is, how do you measure the size
or the scale of the language

64
00:02:53,190 --> 00:02:54,180
model?

65
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:57,280
And they're traditionally
three axes of scale.

66
00:02:57,280 --> 00:02:59,700
So the training FLOPS
or the amount of compute

67
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,280
that you use to train
the language model.

68
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:04,500
The number of model
parameters or the size

69
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:05,460
of the language model.

70
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:07,140
And also the size
of the training data

71
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:09,390
set that the model
is trained on.

72
00:03:09,390 --> 00:03:13,300
And a lot of the plots here
will use either training FLOPS

73
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:15,518
or the number of
model parameters.

74
00:03:15,518 --> 00:03:17,560
The reason is that the
training of the asset size

75
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:20,800
is usually fixed for
different sized models.

76
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,480
And because training
FLOPS is just

77
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:25,750
the data set size times
model of parameters,

78
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:28,390
you can get a similar plot
from either training FLOPS

79
00:03:28,390 --> 00:03:31,375
or a number of model parameters
for most language models.

80
00:03:31,375 --> 00:03:34,550

81
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:35,120
Great.

82
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:38,150
And so the first
type of emergence--

83
00:03:38,150 --> 00:03:41,000
yeah, sorry, go ahead.

84
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:41,750
Yeah.

85
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:44,720
For, me it seems like it
would be relatively easier

86
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:46,010
to measure the size.

87
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:48,410
First it's like what
qualifies as natural.

88
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:54,110
I need to find chances of
natural ability versus not.

89
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:57,020
Yeah, sure--

90
00:03:57,020 --> 00:04:01,190
So yeah, for example,
I'll just give an example

91
00:04:01,190 --> 00:04:03,390
here, which is actually
in the next slide.

92
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:06,200
So basically, we have
this way of interacting

93
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:08,870
with language models,
called few short prompting.

94
00:04:08,870 --> 00:04:12,050
And the way it works is, the
language model is a really good

95
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:14,420
next word predictor.

96
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:18,170
And when you give the
model an example, and then

97
00:04:18,170 --> 00:04:24,060
you ask it for an unseen like
movie review for example.

98
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:25,580
And then you say,
what's the output?

99
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:26,780
And then here,
the language model

100
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:28,460
can say positive,
because it understands

101
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:34,040
to use the context from the
review to give the next token.

102
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:38,840
And the definition that we
use for having ability or not,

103
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:41,840
is that basically, a
few shot prompted task,

104
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,170
for example, sentiment
analysis, is emergent

105
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:47,840
if it has random accuracy
for small models,

106
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:50,545
but above random accuracy
for large models.

107
00:04:50,545 --> 00:04:51,420
Does that make sense?

108
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:54,585
So basically, if the model isn't
doing any better than random,

109
00:04:54,585 --> 00:04:56,210
then we say it doesn't
have the ability

110
00:04:56,210 --> 00:04:57,410
to do this particular task.

111
00:04:57,410 --> 00:05:02,050

112
00:05:02,050 --> 00:05:05,690
I'll give you a
few examples here.

113
00:05:05,690 --> 00:05:09,730
So here's the canonical way that
we look at plots for emergence.

114
00:05:09,730 --> 00:05:14,620
So basically, what we have,
each of these different plots

115
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:18,040
is a different task, and I'll
go over some examples soon.

116
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:19,810
But the way you
read the plot is,

117
00:05:19,810 --> 00:05:22,750
the x-axis is the number
of training FLOPS,

118
00:05:22,750 --> 00:05:24,520
or the model scale.

119
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:26,830
And then, the y-axis
is like the accuracy,

120
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:30,280
or how good the model
is doing the task.

121
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:34,000
And then, we have different
language models from OpenAI,

122
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,280
from Google, and from DeepMind.

123
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:39,470
And then, each of the points
is a different language model.

124
00:05:39,470 --> 00:05:42,280
It's not a language model
over the course of training.

125
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:45,040
Each point is a
different language model.

126
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:49,510
And what you see is that, for
the very small language models,

127
00:05:49,510 --> 00:05:52,810
you basically get a performance
that's close to random,

128
00:05:52,810 --> 00:05:55,990
or not being any
better than random.

129
00:05:55,990 --> 00:05:59,570
And then once you pass
a certain threshold,

130
00:05:59,570 --> 00:06:05,602
you can see that the performance
suddenly gets above random.

131
00:06:05,602 --> 00:06:07,060
And this is what
we call emergence.

132
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:11,230
So basically, if you were
to extrapolate the lines

133
00:06:11,230 --> 00:06:13,120
from the small
language models, you

134
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:16,030
might predict that it would
never do better than random

135
00:06:16,030 --> 00:06:17,668
because it's just a flat line.

136
00:06:17,668 --> 00:06:19,960
But the interesting phenomena
is that when you scale up

137
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:22,120
past a certain
threshold, you actually

138
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:24,340
do see this emergent
phenomenon where the model does

139
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:25,382
a lot better than random.

140
00:06:25,382 --> 00:06:28,690

141
00:06:28,690 --> 00:06:32,330
So let me go over some
concrete examples.

142
00:06:32,330 --> 00:06:34,360
So here's one task.

143
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:40,090
It's basically a benchmark
called multitask NLU or MMLU.

144
00:06:40,090 --> 00:06:43,180
And basically, what
it is, it's a bunch

145
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:46,180
of test questions
ranging from high school

146
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:49,740
all the way to
professional level exams.

147
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:51,900
And how it works is,
the language model

148
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:53,670
is sort of given--

149
00:06:53,670 --> 00:06:57,210
for example, here is a
high school math example.

150
00:06:57,210 --> 00:06:59,820
And the language model
is given a few examples,

151
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:04,080
and then for an unseen question,
it has to give the answer.

152
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:06,580
And then you can see in
the plot on the right.

153
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:10,200
So for the model scale,
if you go up to 10

154
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:12,240
to the power of
22 training slots,

155
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:14,580
you don't actually get any
better than random accuracy

156
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:15,510
on this task.

157
00:07:15,510 --> 00:07:19,170
But if you scale up to 10
to the 24 training FLOPS,

158
00:07:19,170 --> 00:07:22,770
then you see that all the three
models there do much better

159
00:07:22,770 --> 00:07:24,390
than random accuracy.

160
00:07:24,390 --> 00:07:27,580

161
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:28,330
Yeah, go ahead.

162
00:07:28,330 --> 00:07:30,700
The scale of the data
used to train this,

163
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:32,740
is it roughly similar,
or because these

164
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:36,070
are different models
trained by different ports?

165
00:07:36,070 --> 00:07:39,580
Yeah, the scale is, I think,
within an order of magnitude

166
00:07:39,580 --> 00:07:42,370
for these models here, yeah.

167
00:07:42,370 --> 00:07:45,760
And plus, every single dot
on each individual tracks

168
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:47,680
is the same data?

169
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,860
Yes, the data is fixed
except for a chinchilla.

170
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:53,020
Chinchilla it uses more
data for larger models,

171
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:57,045
but I believe, for all the other
models here, the amount of data

172
00:07:57,045 --> 00:07:57,670
stays the same.

173
00:07:57,670 --> 00:08:01,100

174
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:02,700
Yeah, here's just
another example

175
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:05,810
to show it more concretely.

176
00:08:05,810 --> 00:08:10,550
So this is a task from
the BIG-bench benchmark.

177
00:08:10,550 --> 00:08:15,200
Just as an aside, the BIG-bench
benchmark is 200 benchmarks,

178
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:18,140
and basically it's like a
crowdsource set of benchmarks,

179
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:21,260
so I'd recommend looking at if
you're doing [INAUDIBLE] work.

180
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:23,930
And basically, the task
is, the language model

181
00:08:23,930 --> 00:08:26,990
has to take an English
sentence, and then

182
00:08:26,990 --> 00:08:30,590
give the International Phonetic
Alphabet transliteration,

183
00:08:30,590 --> 00:08:32,990
the IPA transliteration,
which is basically,

184
00:08:32,990 --> 00:08:35,960
how to pronounce it.

185
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:38,840
And for this task,
the evaluation metric

186
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:43,190
is actually blue or like
an N-gram overlap metric.

187
00:08:43,190 --> 00:08:46,520
And you got a similar
phenomenon where,

188
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:49,910
as you increase the size
of the language model,

189
00:08:49,910 --> 00:08:53,720
it's flat for a while, and
then suddenly the improvement

190
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:54,560
is above random.

191
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:57,690

192
00:08:57,690 --> 00:08:59,330
Great.

193
00:08:59,330 --> 00:09:03,217
So I'll talk about another
interesting result here

194
00:09:03,217 --> 00:09:04,550
that, that's why it's emergence.

195
00:09:04,550 --> 00:09:07,400
So this was a technical
report that we put out

196
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:09,170
a couple of months ago.

197
00:09:09,170 --> 00:09:13,100
And basically, there's this
really interesting prize in--

198
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:17,660
or it's like a one time
prize in language models

199
00:09:17,660 --> 00:09:21,380
where, Anthropic, which
is like a startup,

200
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,720
basically had this prize
where, if people could come up

201
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:27,470
with a task where the
performance on the task

202
00:09:27,470 --> 00:09:31,010
would actually decrease
as you increase

203
00:09:31,010 --> 00:09:33,560
the size of the
language model, then you

204
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:35,382
would get a bunch of money.

205
00:09:35,382 --> 00:09:37,590
So basically, there are a
lot of submissions to this,

206
00:09:37,590 --> 00:09:40,130
and here's one example
of a task where

207
00:09:40,130 --> 00:09:42,170
they found that the
performance would actually

208
00:09:42,170 --> 00:09:45,110
decrease if you increase the
size of the language model.

209
00:09:45,110 --> 00:09:46,940
So the tasks is--

210
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:49,400
here it's like, repeat
my sense is back to me,

211
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:53,030
and then the input is, all
that glisters is not glue.

212
00:09:53,030 --> 00:09:55,610
And the output is--

213
00:09:55,610 --> 00:10:01,340
the model has to
accurately say a glue.

214
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:06,050
And so what happened is, for
the small language model,

215
00:10:06,050 --> 00:10:09,350
it doesn't know the phrase
all that glisters is not gold.

216
00:10:09,350 --> 00:10:11,660
So it just copies the input
and actually does good.

217
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:14,740
It's like 100% on that.

218
00:10:14,740 --> 00:10:16,870
But then, for the
medium size language

219
00:10:16,870 --> 00:10:20,080
model, what you would see is
that the performance actually

220
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:20,860
decreased.

221
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:23,470
Because the medium size
language model knows the phrase

222
00:10:23,470 --> 00:10:25,390
all that glisters is
not gold, and then it

223
00:10:25,390 --> 00:10:28,875
says gold, which actually is
not what the task asks it to do.

224
00:10:28,875 --> 00:10:29,500
Yeah, go ahead.

225
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,050
Someone on Zoom asked, can
you get a physical estimate

226
00:10:32,050 --> 00:10:33,790
of 10 to 24 FLOPS?

227
00:10:33,790 --> 00:10:38,470
Possibly in terms of training
time or number of years.

228
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:45,790
Yeah, so I think 10 to
the 24 FLOPS is around--

229
00:10:45,790 --> 00:10:48,220
so at Google, we use TPUs.

230
00:10:48,220 --> 00:10:55,740
And one part of TPUs, I believe,
is equal to 4,000 a 110s.

231
00:10:55,740 --> 00:11:00,610
And 10 to the 24 FLOPS is like
two pods for around six weeks

232
00:11:00,610 --> 00:11:02,480
or something like that.

233
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:04,930
So it's a lot of compute
to do the pretraining.

234
00:11:04,930 --> 00:11:07,613

235
00:11:07,613 --> 00:11:10,030
I don't know, but do you guys
remember in chemistry class,

236
00:11:10,030 --> 00:11:12,730
when you'd have moles.

237
00:11:12,730 --> 00:11:14,410
And it would be
like 10 to the 23,

238
00:11:14,410 --> 00:11:16,285
and then you're like--
teacher would be like,

239
00:11:16,285 --> 00:11:18,940
oh, don't even think about
how big this number is.

240
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:21,670
That's like the number of
floating point operations

241
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:26,350
that goes into the pretraining
of some of these models.

242
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:26,850
OK, great.

243
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:32,300
Anyways so basically, the
medium size language model

244
00:11:32,300 --> 00:11:33,330
will actually do worse.

245
00:11:33,330 --> 00:11:33,830
Oh, yeah.

246
00:11:33,830 --> 00:11:35,650
Did you have another question?

247
00:11:35,650 --> 00:11:37,752
Yeah.

248
00:11:37,752 --> 00:11:40,680
Does this one the price or not?

249
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:43,650
This one is one of the--

250
00:11:43,650 --> 00:11:47,190
I think it's the third
place winner or something.

251
00:11:47,190 --> 00:11:48,660
[INAUDIBLE]

252
00:11:48,660 --> 00:11:50,580

253
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:51,910
Because I would think--

254
00:11:51,910 --> 00:11:53,740
my initial opinion
would be, oh, you

