00:11:53,740 --> 00:11:57,457
can just put a negative sign
on how you evaluate your tests

256
00:11:57,457 --> 00:11:58,650
[INAUDIBLE]

257
00:11:58,650 --> 00:12:00,420
What do you mean
flip a negative sign?

258
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:03,240
Well, because all of
this depends on which

259
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:07,770
evaluation scheme you use to
measure and you do your test.

260
00:12:07,770 --> 00:12:11,380
So it's like the
measurement is very sparse,

261
00:12:11,380 --> 00:12:15,000
you only get credit if you
do it perfectly or something.

262
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,860
So a lot of things emerge.

263
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:20,760
Because you just won't
get it perfectly.

264
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:22,770
Really optimize it
for a long time.

265
00:12:22,770 --> 00:12:26,610
Or if you take a test,
and then like evaluate,

266
00:12:26,610 --> 00:12:28,320
it's like it's minus sing.

267
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:31,900
Wouldn't it get worse?

268
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:33,750
Like something
that's [INAUDIBLE]..

269
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:35,128
Yeah.

270
00:12:35,128 --> 00:12:37,170
So for this thing, they
like to count it for all.

271
00:12:37,170 --> 00:12:38,880
You can't just say
the task should be

272
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:40,500
able to do badly on something.

273
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:43,390
It has to be a
meaningful sort of task.

274
00:12:43,390 --> 00:12:45,630
And then, I guess
your point about how

275
00:12:45,630 --> 00:12:48,390
[INAUDIBLE] or the
valuation metric works

276
00:12:48,390 --> 00:12:51,090
is actually a really good one.

277
00:12:51,090 --> 00:12:54,315
So I guess it still
kind of counts if--

278
00:12:54,315 --> 00:13:00,420
I guess the argument is,
the performance might not

279
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:03,180
look emergent if you
assign partial credit.

280
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:05,190
But we have a bunch of--

281
00:13:05,190 --> 00:13:08,580
I can show example later, but
even if you use partial credit

282
00:13:08,580 --> 00:13:11,830
metrics, you'll often still
see the same type of emergence.

283
00:13:11,830 --> 00:13:15,230
So it's not purely a phenomenon
of not assigning partial credit

284
00:13:15,230 --> 00:13:16,480
based on the valuation metric.

285
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:22,140

286
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:24,540
And then-- great.

287
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:28,240
So what we argued in this
paper is that, yeah, there

288
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:30,240
might be some tasks where
the performance starts

289
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:33,660
to decrease if you use a
medium size language model.

290
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:38,280
But if you keep scaling all
the way to the largest model

291
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:42,150
that we have at Google
that's known publicly.

292
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:43,470
PaLM.

293
00:13:43,470 --> 00:13:46,290
You'll see that the
language model can actually

294
00:13:46,290 --> 00:13:47,760
go back and do the
task correctly.

295
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:52,200
Because the large language
model also knows the phrase

296
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:56,250
all that glisters is not
gold, but it also understands

297
00:13:56,250 --> 00:13:58,410
repeat my senses back to me.

298
00:13:58,410 --> 00:14:00,990
So it's able to get
100% on this task.

299
00:14:00,990 --> 00:14:04,020
So this is a different
type of emergence also.

300
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:08,290

301
00:14:08,290 --> 00:14:13,420
And another class of emergence
that we talk about in the paper

302
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:15,940
is an emergent
prompting technique.

303
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:19,270
So basically, other than
[INAUDIBLE] prompting,

304
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:21,100
there's other ways
of interacting

305
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:26,230
with the language models, that
can be considered emergent.

306
00:14:26,230 --> 00:14:27,040
Yeah?

307
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:28,498
Sorry, somebody
else had a question

308
00:14:28,498 --> 00:14:30,850
on the previous slides.

309
00:14:30,850 --> 00:14:33,790
The question is, did all
models undergo instruction fine

310
00:14:33,790 --> 00:14:35,740
tuning?

311
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:38,680
None of these models
underwent instruction fine

312
00:14:38,680 --> 00:14:40,190
tuning for this plot.

313
00:14:40,190 --> 00:14:40,690
Yeah.

314
00:14:40,690 --> 00:14:44,590

315
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:46,863
Great.

316
00:14:46,863 --> 00:14:49,030
Yeah, so one way of interacting
with language models

317
00:14:49,030 --> 00:14:53,710
is by basically
finding the model using

318
00:14:53,710 --> 00:14:55,330
a technique called RLHF.

319
00:14:55,330 --> 00:14:56,920
And basically the
way it works is,

320
00:14:56,920 --> 00:15:00,670
you have this data, and humans
rate preferences for what

321
00:15:00,670 --> 00:15:02,620
type of outputs they prefer.

322
00:15:02,620 --> 00:15:05,560
And then the model is
trained on how to optimize

323
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:08,800
for human preferences.

324
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:10,690
And what this plot
is showing here,

325
00:15:10,690 --> 00:15:14,260
is that, if you do
this RLHF on the model,

326
00:15:14,260 --> 00:15:17,620
the model performance on
a different zero-shot task

327
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:19,600
actually gets worse
for small models.

328
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:22,780
You can see the blue line
is above the orange line.

329
00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:26,880
Blue line is the baseline,
the orange line is RLHF.

330
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:28,830
And then, if you do
it for large models,

331
00:15:28,830 --> 00:15:32,100
though, then you can see
that the performance actually

332
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:34,370
has a positive delta
from doing RLHF.

333
00:15:34,370 --> 00:15:37,027

334
00:15:37,027 --> 00:15:39,110
And so this is sort of an
interesting thing, where

335
00:15:39,110 --> 00:15:41,457
a certain technique
might only help

336
00:15:41,457 --> 00:15:43,290
if you try on a large
enough language model.

337
00:15:43,290 --> 00:15:46,220
So if you only try to on
the small language models,

338
00:15:46,220 --> 00:15:49,460
it'd be tough to draw
the conclusion that it

339
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:51,303
wouldn't help performance.

340
00:15:51,303 --> 00:15:53,720
And then later, I'll talk about
chain of thought prompting

341
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:55,490
as another emergent problem.

342
00:15:55,490 --> 00:15:58,660

343
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,420
So here's sort of the
hand-waving diagram

344
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:05,260
that I used, to think about
emergence as a framework.

345
00:16:05,260 --> 00:16:09,810
So on the x-axis here, there's
a scale of the language model.

346
00:16:09,810 --> 00:16:11,190
And on the y-axis,
there's a sort

347
00:16:11,190 --> 00:16:18,550
of imaginary range of things
that a language model can do.

348
00:16:18,550 --> 00:16:20,680
And then basically, you
can pick some random point,

349
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:23,530
like say, 100 billion parameters
in the language model.

350
00:16:23,530 --> 00:16:26,140
And there will be
certain abilities.

351
00:16:26,140 --> 00:16:28,150
OK, so first you can
see, as you increase

352
00:16:28,150 --> 00:16:31,830
the size of the language model,
the number of tasks or things

353
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:33,700
the language model
can do increases.

354
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:38,140
And then you can see,
there are some tasks where

355
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:40,610
model's above 100 billion
parameters, for example,

356
00:16:40,610 --> 00:16:43,510
can do them, but models below
100 parameters can't do them,

357
00:16:43,510 --> 00:16:46,727
and we call these
emerging abilities

358
00:16:46,727 --> 00:16:48,310
Sorry, quick question
about the slide.

359
00:16:48,310 --> 00:16:50,480
What are the colors?

360
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:53,090
Oh, it's just highlighting--

361
00:16:53,090 --> 00:16:56,900
the dark blue is tasks
that a smaller language

362
00:16:56,900 --> 00:16:58,725
model wouldn't be able to do.

363
00:16:58,725 --> 00:16:59,600
Does that make sense?

364
00:16:59,600 --> 00:16:59,990
Yeah.

365
00:16:59,990 --> 00:17:01,760
And then, to the right
of the dotted line,

366
00:17:01,760 --> 00:17:04,780
the white region of top?

367
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:07,420
Oh, that just
means tasks that we

368
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:11,960
haven't been able to solve yet
with language models, yeah.

369
00:17:11,960 --> 00:17:14,089
And I'm curious to
know, do you think

370
00:17:14,089 --> 00:17:16,790
that those tasks in the
white region are unsolvable,

371
00:17:16,790 --> 00:17:19,280
like 100 billion scale.

372
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:24,290
Or do you think that better
models, specific training data

373
00:17:24,290 --> 00:17:26,359
would allow us to have
100 billion scale get

374
00:17:26,359 --> 00:17:27,890
into that white region.

375
00:17:27,890 --> 00:17:34,520
Yeah, I definitely think
that it's not a fixed--

376
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:39,497
I'll give an example
shortly, but it's not a rule

377
00:17:39,497 --> 00:17:41,330
that you have to have
100 billion parameters

378
00:17:41,330 --> 00:17:42,890
to do a certain task.

379
00:17:42,890 --> 00:17:45,440
It's just that that happens
to be the threshold that we've

380
00:17:45,440 --> 00:17:46,940
observed in models so far.

381
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:50,270
And I do think, with better
training data and architecture

382
00:17:50,270 --> 00:17:52,640
and algorithms, we can
probably beat that.

383
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:55,710

384
00:17:55,710 --> 00:17:57,540
Cool.

385
00:17:57,540 --> 00:18:00,000
Yeah, so as [INAUDIBLE]
just mentioned,

386
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:04,203
one example of getting emergence
can be with better data,

387
00:18:04,203 --> 00:18:05,370
so it's not all about scale.

388
00:18:05,370 --> 00:18:06,820
I'll give some [INAUDIBLE] here.

389
00:18:06,820 --> 00:18:09,930
So for this task--
this is just one

390
00:18:09,930 --> 00:18:12,300
of the tasks in the
BIG-bench benchmark.

391
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:15,060
You can see that for LaMDA,
which is a Google model,

392
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:18,960
and GPT-3, you actually don't
get emergence from scaling

393
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:25,770
to 137 or 175
billion parameters.

394
00:18:25,770 --> 00:18:29,040
But when you come in with a
different language model, PaLM,

395
00:18:29,040 --> 00:18:32,940
which is trained on better
data than LaMDA and GPT-3,

396
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,280
you actually can get
this emergent ability

397
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:37,290
even with the smaller
language model, so in here

398
00:18:37,290 --> 00:18:40,240
at 62 billion parameters.

399
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:44,790
[INAUDIBLE] better model has
better data, or also better

400
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:47,130
architectural [INAUDIBLE]?

401
00:18:47,130 --> 00:18:49,860

402
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:55,110
Yeah, so the challenging thing
is-- that's a great question.

403
00:18:55,110 --> 00:18:57,660
There's a lot of differences
between PaLM and LaMDA,

404
00:18:57,660 --> 00:18:58,560
for example.

405
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:01,710
And we can't really ablate them
in any controlled way because

406
00:19:01,710 --> 00:19:03,930
of the cost of pretraining.

407
00:19:03,930 --> 00:19:06,882
But our running
hypothesis is that PaLM

408
00:19:06,882 --> 00:19:08,340
is trained on better
data, and that

409
00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:11,370
accounts for a lot of the
difference between PaLM

410
00:19:11,370 --> 00:19:12,030
and LaMDA.

411
00:19:12,030 --> 00:19:16,380
I seen the smaller scales where
it is possible to ablate stuff?

412
00:19:16,380 --> 00:19:17,580
Really architecture wise.

413
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:20,130
[INAUDIBLE]

414
00:19:20,130 --> 00:19:21,400
Yeah, that's a good question.

415
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:23,025
So I guess, even
here, you can look at,

416
00:19:23,025 --> 00:19:25,860
for example, the
PaLM 8-billion model.

417
00:19:25,860 --> 00:19:27,030
Like that.

418
00:19:27,030 --> 00:19:28,680
That point there.

419
00:19:28,680 --> 00:19:30,900
You can ablate it, and
it's a little bit higher,

420
00:19:30,900 --> 00:19:33,390
but it's not really an
emergent yet at that point.

421
00:19:33,390 --> 00:19:37,200
So it's hard to tell, for
example, this particular task,

422
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:39,300
what the effect is.

423
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:41,250
There's a question on Zoom.

424
00:19:41,250 --> 00:19:43,080
Are there two different
versions of PaLM?

425
00:19:43,080 --> 00:19:45,750
If not, why are there
two lines for it?

426
00:19:45,750 --> 00:19:48,070
Oh, so I think the
two lines here.

427
00:19:48,070 --> 00:19:54,187
One is maybe three shot,
and then one is zero shot.

428
00:19:54,187 --> 00:19:55,020
Something like that.

429
00:19:55,020 --> 00:19:58,298
So it just refers to
the way that we're

430
00:19:58,298 --> 00:19:59,340
using the language model.

431
00:19:59,340 --> 00:20:00,930
Either with or
without exemplars.

432
00:20:00,930 --> 00:20:04,810

433
00:20:04,810 --> 00:20:07,160
Great.

434
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:11,220
I'll talk about a small
ablation here that shows this.

435
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:15,110
So this is an ablation
on a toy task,

436
00:20:15,110 --> 00:20:18,890
where basically, the language
model has to know that.

437
00:20:18,890 --> 00:20:22,070
In English, you have to use
plural verbs with plural

438
00:20:22,070 --> 00:20:24,785
subjects, and singular verbs
with singular subjects.

439
00:20:24,785 --> 00:20:28,880

440
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:30,470
What we're doing
here is basically,

441
00:20:30,470 --> 00:20:33,830
we train these small
verb models from scratch,

442
00:20:33,830 --> 00:20:36,290
and then we held out--

443
00:20:36,290 --> 00:20:39,080
we fixed the frequency
of certain verbs

444
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:40,220
in a training data set.

445
00:20:40,220 --> 00:20:42,620
Which basically says,
OK, what's the effect

446
00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:47,200
of seeing a certain verb
in the data more often?

447
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:50,800
In this plot, the x-axis is
like the frequency of the verb,

448
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:53,140
and the y-axis is
the error rate.

449
00:20:53,140 --> 00:20:56,200
And what you basically
see is that, if you

450
00:20:56,200 --> 00:20:57,440
have more in-domain data.

451
00:20:57,440 --> 00:20:59,980
So if the model sees
the verb more times,

452
00:20:59,980 --> 00:21:02,140
it does the task a lot better.

453
00:21:02,140 --> 00:21:05,440
This is an example of having
high-quality data, or data

454
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:08,827
that's more in-domain for
the task evaluating on,

455
00:21:08,827 --> 00:21:09,910
can make a big difference.

456
00:21:09,910 --> 00:21:13,450
Even if you're fixing the
compute, the size of the model,

457
00:21:13,450 --> 00:21:16,230
and the rest of the data.

458
00:21:16,230 --> 00:21:17,090
Question on Zoom.

459
00:21:17,090 --> 00:21:18,740
Someone asks, could
there be a way

460
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:20,600
to distill convergent
abilities down

461
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:24,470
to smaller models from
larger teacher models?

462
00:21:24,470 --> 00:21:25,550
Yeah, I think so.

463
00:21:25,550 --> 00:21:31,240
So larger teacher
models can basically--

464
00:21:31,240 --> 00:21:34,640
you can use them, for
example, to generate data.

465
00:21:34,640 --> 00:21:38,740
And then if you fine tune
the smaller model on data,

466
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:42,100
it's pretty likely that you'll
be able to get the ability

467
00:21:42,100 --> 00:21:43,778
to emerge in the smaller model.

468
00:21:43,778 --> 00:21:45,320
I'll talk about
example of this, too.

469
00:21:45,320 --> 00:21:46,420
Let me see.

470
00:21:46,420 --> 00:21:49,740
Oh actually that's
next slide, so--

471
00:21:49,740 --> 00:21:52,230
Desired behaviors can be
induced in smaller models

472
00:21:52,230 --> 00:21:55,210
once you know what
behavior you want.

473
00:21:55,210 --> 00:22:02,760
So for example, here's a
figure from this ChatGPT paper.

474
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:05,130
And basically, the
desired behavior

475
00:22:05,130 --> 00:22:07,860
here is instruction following.

476
00:22:07,860 --> 00:22:10,810
And you can see that
there's multiple models.

477
00:22:10,810 --> 00:22:13,050
So on the left you
have these small models

478
00:22:13,050 --> 00:22:15,750
that are trained with
RLHF, and they actually

479
00:22:15,750 --> 00:22:18,630
have better performance
than larger models

480
00:22:18,630 --> 00:22:21,240
trained on weaker techniques.

481
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:22,740
So basically the
point is, if you

482
00:22:22,740 --> 00:22:26,040
know that you want a
certain behavior that you

483
00:22:26,040 --> 00:22:30,700
saw previously in an emergent
way in a larger model,

484
00:22:30,700 --> 00:22:32,700
you can find a way to
fine tune on that behavior

485
00:22:32,700 --> 00:22:38,070
specifically, and induce that
behavior in a smaller model.

486
00:22:38,070 --> 00:22:39,530
But I guess, one
of the limitations

487
00:22:39,530 --> 00:22:43,260
is that, unless you know all
the behaviors that you want,

488
00:22:43,260 --> 00:22:45,635
you can't really get this
natural emergent behavior.

489
00:22:45,635 --> 00:22:50,700

490
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:54,441
Yeah, another discussion
point here is that--

491
00:22:54,441 --> 00:22:57,810
there's this question of, what's
the right x-axis for emergence?

492
00:22:57,810 --> 00:23:00,990
So right now, we mostly
talk about model parameters

493
00:23:00,990 --> 00:23:02,010
and training FLOPS.

494
00:23:02,010 --> 00:23:06,150
But I guess, if you ask DeepMind
people how they look at it,

495
00:23:06,150 --> 00:23:09,630
you'll get this argument that
model parameters and training

496
00:23:09,630 --> 00:23:13,770
FLOPS are really just a proxy
for how good the model is.

497
00:23:13,770 --> 00:23:15,420
And how good the
model is can really

498
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:20,430
be measured by perplexity or
how well it's doing on some dev

499
00:23:20,430 --> 00:23:22,920
sets, which is Wiki-text 103.

500
00:23:22,920 --> 00:23:27,180
So basically, you can
also measure emergence

501
00:23:27,180 --> 00:23:29,130
in terms of perplexity.

502
00:23:29,130 --> 00:23:33,600
So here is Wiki-text perplexity.

503
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:35,670
And then you can see,
on a downstream task

504
00:23:35,670 --> 00:23:38,280
that, as the
perplexity gets better,

505
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:41,370
there's this threshold where
are you able to do a lot better

506
00:23:41,370 --> 00:23:44,520
on the downstream task.

507
00:23:44,520 --> 00:23:47,400
And there's a
strong correlation,

508
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:49,590
at least right now, between
perplexity and training

509
00:23:49,590 --> 00:23:50,090
compute.

510
00:23:50,090 --> 00:23:52,950
So you can see these two
lines are pretty similar.

511
00:23:52,950 --> 00:23:55,730

512
00:23:55,730 --> 00:23:57,920
Basically, I think,
in the future

513
00:23:57,920 --> 00:23:59,735
if we have much
better models that

514
00:23:59,735 --> 00:24:01,610
are a lot smaller, churn
out much better data

515
00:24:01,610 --> 00:24:04,970
and better algorithms, then
maybe Wiki-text perplexity can

516
00:24:04,970 --> 00:24:09,290
show a different type of plot
than using other metrics.

517
00:24:09,290 --> 00:24:11,970

518
00:24:11,970 --> 00:24:16,800
So Wiki-text is basically a--

519
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:19,650
I think it's a
subset of Wikipedia.

520
00:24:19,650 --> 00:24:22,800
And then perplexity is a measure
of how well you can predict

521
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:25,730
the next word in a data set.

522
00:24:25,730 --> 00:24:28,460
So basically, if you're really
good at modeling the next word

523
00:24:28,460 --> 00:24:32,600
on this particular
evaluation set,

524
00:24:32,600 --> 00:24:37,180
that's a measure of how well
you understand language.

525
00:24:37,180 --> 00:24:38,230
That make sense?

526
00:24:38,230 --> 00:24:40,180
[INAUDIBLE]

527
00:24:40,180 --> 00:24:41,260

528
00:24:41,260 --> 00:24:43,290
Oh, this is like a
held out test set.

529
00:24:43,290 --> 00:24:50,370

530
00:24:50,370 --> 00:24:52,290
And then, a final
thing that I think

531
00:24:52,290 --> 00:24:54,900
is pretty exciting
about emergence,

532
00:24:54,900 --> 00:24:58,080
is that they're sort of not
just technical emergence

533
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:00,090
that we've talked
about, but there's

534
00:25:00,090 --> 00:25:04,680
sociological changes in how
the AI community views scaling

535
00:25:04,680 --> 00:25:07,630
and how to use language models.

536
00:25:07,630 --> 00:25:11,810
So here are some
examples of where

537
00:25:11,810 --> 00:25:15,500
scaling up the size of the
language model enables you to--

538
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:17,900
the sort of few-shot scenario.

539
00:25:17,900 --> 00:25:20,900
Beat a task-specific,
fine-tuned language model

540
00:25:20,900 --> 00:25:25,200
that's usually fine tuned on
say, thousands of examples.

541
00:25:25,200 --> 00:25:28,370
So basically, the green line
is the prior state-of-the-art

542
00:25:28,370 --> 00:25:32,420
achieved by fine tuning.

543
00:25:32,420 --> 00:25:34,100
And then the blue
dots basically show,

544
00:25:34,100 --> 00:25:36,337
if you take a pretrained
language model and you do

545
00:25:36,337 --> 00:25:38,420
few-shot prompting, which
means the language model

546
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:40,752
isn't intentionally
trained to do the task,

547
00:25:40,752 --> 00:25:42,710
you can often get
state-of-the-art results just

548
00:25:42,710 --> 00:25:45,950
by continuing to scale up the
size of the language model.

549
00:25:45,950 --> 00:25:48,000
And obviously, there's
limitations here,

550
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,450
you don't want to just keep
scaling up in order to get

551
00:25:50,450 --> 00:25:51,620
state-of-the-art.

552
00:25:51,620 --> 00:25:54,590
But I think it's a pretty
big change in people's minds,

553
00:25:54,590 --> 00:25:56,838
that you could actually get
some of the best results

554
00:25:56,838 --> 00:25:58,880
just by scaling up the
size of the language model

555
00:25:58,880 --> 00:26:02,430
and doing prompting.

556
00:26:02,430 --> 00:26:03,410
Question from Zoom.

557
00:26:03,410 --> 00:26:06,450
Someone asks, is that
