00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:39,420
so it depends on
your particular task.

569
00:26:39,420 --> 00:26:50,520
But what this plot is saying is
that fine-tuned smaller models

570
00:26:50,520 --> 00:26:53,040
can do well on some tasks
if you target it well.

571
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:56,140
But for like tasks
are more complicated,

572
00:26:56,140 --> 00:26:58,380
often you can do
better just by scaling.

573
00:26:58,380 --> 00:27:01,230
So there's sort
of tasks that fall

574
00:27:01,230 --> 00:27:03,090
into both of these categories.

575
00:27:03,090 --> 00:27:06,520
And I wouldn't say that
it's contradictory.

576
00:27:06,520 --> 00:27:13,270
I guess some tasks, you would do
a lot better just by scaling up

577
00:27:13,270 --> 00:27:14,460
the size of the model.

578
00:27:14,460 --> 00:27:17,610
And then other tasks, if
it's a very narrow domain

579
00:27:17,610 --> 00:27:20,100
or the large language
model might not

580
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:22,018
be trained on that
kind of data, then you

581
00:27:22,018 --> 00:27:23,310
would do better by fine tuning.

582
00:27:23,310 --> 00:27:27,070

583
00:27:27,070 --> 00:27:29,150
OK, great.

584
00:27:29,150 --> 00:27:31,302
So here's a little
summary slide.

585
00:27:31,302 --> 00:27:33,010
So basically, emergent
abilities can only

586
00:27:33,010 --> 00:27:34,510
be observed in large models.

587
00:27:34,510 --> 00:27:38,225
And if you try to predict
their emergence just by looking

588
00:27:38,225 --> 00:27:39,850
at the plots for
small models, then you

589
00:27:39,850 --> 00:27:42,670
wouldn't be able to do it.

590
00:27:42,670 --> 00:27:46,870
And of had a little reflection
on how to look at this.

591
00:27:46,870 --> 00:27:48,520
So emergence is
really this framing

592
00:27:48,520 --> 00:27:52,960
of how to view new abilities
that are not intentionally

593
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:55,090
built in to the pretraining.

594
00:27:55,090 --> 00:27:57,470
And I think the subtext for
this is super important,

595
00:27:57,470 --> 00:28:00,550
which is, you can see it as
implicit argument for why

596
00:28:00,550 --> 00:28:03,032
we should keep scaling
up language models

597
00:28:03,032 --> 00:28:04,990
because you get these
abilities that are really

598
00:28:04,990 --> 00:28:07,342
hard to find otherwise.

599
00:28:07,342 --> 00:28:09,300
And the context around
this is pretty important

600
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:13,320
because it's really
expensive to continue scaling

601
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:14,070
up these models.

602
00:28:14,070 --> 00:28:17,910
And even one year
ago, a lot of people

603
00:28:17,910 --> 00:28:20,370
didn't believe that you could
do better on certain tasks

604
00:28:20,370 --> 00:28:22,453
just by scaling up the
size of the language model.

605
00:28:22,453 --> 00:28:24,685

606
00:28:24,685 --> 00:28:26,310
If you work in industry
at all, there's

607
00:28:26,310 --> 00:28:29,670
this interesting tension
between emergence and also

608
00:28:29,670 --> 00:28:30,990
like many production tasks.

609
00:28:30,990 --> 00:28:33,480
So emergence is
sort of this task

610
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:36,630
general phenomena where
you scale up the model

611
00:28:36,630 --> 00:28:38,670
and it's really expensive,
but the single model

612
00:28:38,670 --> 00:28:40,770
can do a lot of tasks.

613
00:28:40,770 --> 00:28:44,100
In the direction of AGI.

614
00:28:44,100 --> 00:28:46,260
And then, for many
production tasks,

615
00:28:46,260 --> 00:28:50,220
you have the opposite where
you know what task it is.

616
00:28:50,220 --> 00:28:52,770
For example, translating
into Spanish.

617
00:28:52,770 --> 00:28:54,780
And then you have these
constraints on compute.

618
00:28:54,780 --> 00:28:57,267
Because when you build Google
Translate, for example,

619
00:28:57,267 --> 00:28:59,850
you don't want people to have
to wait a couple of seconds just

620
00:28:59,850 --> 00:29:02,230
to get the translation.

621
00:29:02,230 --> 00:29:05,160
And then, you also happen to
have a lot of in-domain data.

622
00:29:05,160 --> 00:29:08,760
So you have, for
example, a million pairs

623
00:29:08,760 --> 00:29:12,150
