Yeah, so basically,
what we did is,

765
00:35:48,930 --> 00:35:51,030
there's 200 tasks in BIG bench.

766
00:35:51,030 --> 00:35:52,650
And then we basically
classified them

767
00:35:52,650 --> 00:35:58,350
into smoothly increasing,
emergent with GPT-3 or LaMDA,

768
00:35:58,350 --> 00:36:00,210
emergent with PaLM,
and then flat,

769
00:36:00,210 --> 00:36:02,260
which is no model
better than random.

770
00:36:02,260 --> 00:36:06,000
So I think, if you look at
any of these tasks here,

771
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:08,890
they should still
not have emerged yet.

772
00:36:08,890 --> 00:36:12,450
And if you can get them to
emerge, that'd be interesting.

773
00:36:12,450 --> 00:36:15,030

774
00:36:15,030 --> 00:36:16,080
Sorry?

775
00:36:16,080 --> 00:36:18,675
I think ChatGPT could
be 20 questions.

776
00:36:18,675 --> 00:36:20,537
Oh, OK, yeah, this
is not a super--

777
00:36:20,537 --> 00:36:22,370
I think this is a couple
of months old, so--

778
00:36:22,370 --> 00:36:23,360
Oh, sorry.

779
00:36:23,360 --> 00:36:24,830
Yeah, yeah.

780
00:36:24,830 --> 00:36:26,260
Oh, 20 questions?

781
00:36:26,260 --> 00:36:27,562
OK, yeah.

782
00:36:27,562 --> 00:36:29,530
[INAUDIBLE]

783
00:36:29,530 --> 00:36:32,580
Yeah, I think--
the cool thing is,

784
00:36:32,580 --> 00:36:34,020
you can see over time, right?

785
00:36:34,020 --> 00:36:37,163
Originally, maybe only
these were emerging.

786
00:36:37,163 --> 00:36:38,580
And then when PaLM
came out, you'd

787
00:36:38,580 --> 00:36:41,290
see a couple of dozen more
abilities emerging and then--

788
00:36:41,290 --> 00:36:45,600
I suspect, in a year
or two, most of these

789
00:36:45,600 --> 00:36:48,780
will become emergent, and
we'll need harder benchmarks.

790
00:36:48,780 --> 00:36:49,530
Yeah?

791
00:36:49,530 --> 00:36:50,905
There's another
question on Zoom.

792
00:36:50,905 --> 00:36:54,090
Why doesn't Google take as much
of a safety-centric approach,

793
00:36:54,090 --> 00:36:55,830
like you said in Anthropic does?

794
00:36:55,830 --> 00:36:58,680
Are there reasons to believe
harmful capabilities wouldn't

795
00:36:58,680 --> 00:36:59,280
be emergent?

796
00:36:59,280 --> 00:37:02,800

797
00:37:02,800 --> 00:37:05,560
Yeah, I don't want to answer the
question on behalf of Google,

798
00:37:05,560 --> 00:37:10,360
I just can only talk
about my own opinions.

799
00:37:10,360 --> 00:37:12,900
But I think the
reality is that Google,

800
00:37:12,900 --> 00:37:15,400
even if you look at the amount
of research that Google does,

801
00:37:15,400 --> 00:37:18,640
it might not be in the
large language model space

802
00:37:18,640 --> 00:37:19,240
specifically.

803
00:37:19,240 --> 00:37:22,640
But the amount of safety
research that we do, I think,

804
00:37:22,640 --> 00:37:24,520
is more than Anthropic,
if you actually

805
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:26,530
look at the number
of papers published.

806
00:37:26,530 --> 00:37:31,300
Don't quote me on this,
but I think that's correct.

807
00:37:31,300 --> 00:37:31,800
OK.

808
00:37:31,800 --> 00:37:34,265

809
00:37:34,265 --> 00:37:34,765
Great.

810
00:37:34,765 --> 00:37:37,800

811
00:37:37,800 --> 00:37:42,373
So yeah, I'll talk about
chain-of-thought prompting.

812
00:37:42,373 --> 00:37:44,040
So basically,
chain-of-thought prompting

813
00:37:44,040 --> 00:37:47,700
is this way of doing
reasoning, multi-step reasoning

814
00:37:47,700 --> 00:37:49,890
with large language models.

815
00:37:49,890 --> 00:37:53,970
And yeah, I wanted
to say that I get

816
00:37:53,970 --> 00:37:56,310
super exciting to
see a lot of people

817
00:37:56,310 --> 00:38:00,600
at Google working on this, and
also to see Sundar, our CEO,

818
00:38:00,600 --> 00:38:04,320
present this at our last
year's Google I/O press event.

819
00:38:04,320 --> 00:38:06,900

820
00:38:06,900 --> 00:38:10,380
And basically, the
motivation for this

821
00:38:10,380 --> 00:38:14,580
is that we want language models
to do more complicated tasks

822
00:38:14,580 --> 00:38:15,180
that--

823
00:38:15,180 --> 00:38:17,820
for example, we
know language models

824
00:38:17,820 --> 00:38:19,920
can do easy tasks like
[INAUDIBLE] analysis

825
00:38:19,920 --> 00:38:20,940
or translation.

826
00:38:20,940 --> 00:38:23,160
But what about more
complicated tasks

827
00:38:23,160 --> 00:38:28,360
that might even take a human
a minute or more to do?

828
00:38:28,360 --> 00:38:30,300
And the goal here is
to basically guide them

829
00:38:30,300 --> 00:38:31,980
with metadata.

830
00:38:31,980 --> 00:38:35,220
So for example, instead of just
giving an input-output pair,

831
00:38:35,220 --> 00:38:38,280
we want to give them the
entire reasoning process,

832
00:38:38,280 --> 00:38:40,930
and have them mimic that.

833
00:38:40,930 --> 00:38:45,100
And basically, you can see
here, in a standard prompt,

834
00:38:45,100 --> 00:38:46,968
you have the question
and then the answer,

835
00:38:46,968 --> 00:38:48,760
and then you have a
question, and the model

836
00:38:48,760 --> 00:38:49,960
gives a new answer.

837
00:38:49,960 --> 00:38:52,900
Unfortunately it's wrong.

838
00:38:52,900 --> 00:38:56,380
And then, with
chain-of-thought prompting,

839
00:38:56,380 --> 00:38:58,690
you give the model
a question, and then

840
00:38:58,690 --> 00:39:02,000
like how your teacher would
ask you to show your work,

841
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:04,330
you give the chain
of thought, is

842
00:39:04,330 --> 00:39:06,790
what we call it, or
basically, a reasoning path.

843
00:39:06,790 --> 00:39:08,410
And then you give
the final answer.

844
00:39:08,410 --> 00:39:10,900
And then when the model
sees this unseen question,

845
00:39:10,900 --> 00:39:12,610
now it's able to give
the reasoning path,

846
00:39:12,610 --> 00:39:15,490
and then give the
correct final answer.

847
00:39:15,490 --> 00:39:18,610
And the way that we add these
prompts into the prompt,

848
00:39:18,610 --> 00:39:21,010
is basically, we just
manually write a couple,

849
00:39:21,010 --> 00:39:23,890
and then add it into the prompt.

850
00:39:23,890 --> 00:39:27,040
So let me just show
how that works.

851
00:39:27,040 --> 00:39:29,920

852
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:32,750
So this is the OpenAI API.

853
00:39:32,750 --> 00:39:38,490
And basically, here's the
non-chain-of-thought way

854
00:39:38,490 --> 00:39:39,010
of doing it.

855
00:39:39,010 --> 00:39:43,480
So basically, you would have
a question, answer, question,

856
00:39:43,480 --> 00:39:44,730
answer, question, answer.

857
00:39:44,730 --> 00:39:46,920
And then a new question about--

858
00:39:46,920 --> 00:39:48,625
cafeteria has 23
apples, they use 20

859
00:39:48,625 --> 00:39:50,000
to make lunch and
about six more.

860
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:52,080
How many apples they have?

861
00:39:52,080 --> 00:39:56,260
And the model gets it wrong.

862
00:39:56,260 --> 00:39:58,810
And the only difference
with chain of thought

863
00:39:58,810 --> 00:40:03,850
is that you give these
intermediate reasoning paths

864
00:40:03,850 --> 00:40:05,360
before giving the final answer.

865
00:40:05,360 --> 00:40:06,640
So here's a path.

866
00:40:06,640 --> 00:40:08,650
There's a reasoning chain.

867
00:40:08,650 --> 00:40:10,570
There's another reasoning chain.

868
00:40:10,570 --> 00:40:15,850
And then, now the model
for this unseen question

869
00:40:15,850 --> 00:40:18,880
gives the entire
reasoning process.

870
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:23,210
And then this actually enables
the models to get it correct.

871
00:40:23,210 --> 00:40:26,750
I'll give another quick example.

872
00:40:26,750 --> 00:40:28,370
This one.

873
00:40:28,370 --> 00:40:31,235
So here the task is, just
take the last letters

874
00:40:31,235 --> 00:40:32,360
of the words of Bill Gates.

875
00:40:32,360 --> 00:40:35,270
So L from Bill and S from gates.

876
00:40:35,270 --> 00:40:36,540
And then concatenate them.

877
00:40:36,540 --> 00:40:39,740
And the answer should be LS.

878
00:40:39,740 --> 00:40:42,140
And then here, the
model gets it wrong.

879
00:40:42,140 --> 00:40:44,610
The answer should be NK.

880
00:40:44,610 --> 00:40:46,870
It says SK.

881
00:40:46,870 --> 00:40:50,110
And then, if you do
chain of thought,

882
00:40:50,110 --> 00:40:53,690
obviously it becomes
very easy for the model.

883
00:40:53,690 --> 00:40:56,390
So it says the last third
of Bill L, the last letter

884
00:40:56,390 --> 00:41:00,810
of Gates is S. Answer is LS.

885
00:41:00,810 --> 00:41:03,960
And then here, it's able to do,
the last letter of Elon is N,

886
00:41:03,960 --> 00:41:06,720
and the last letter of
Musk is K, and answers NK.

887
00:41:06,720 --> 00:41:10,750

888
00:41:10,750 --> 00:41:12,000
Is this clear?

889
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:15,300
Any questions about
what's going on here?

890
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:15,800
OK.

891
00:41:15,800 --> 00:41:18,930

892
00:41:18,930 --> 00:41:21,720
So basically, we can have
these similar plots where

893
00:41:21,720 --> 00:41:26,530
the x-axis is the model scale,
the y-axis is the performance.

894
00:41:26,530 --> 00:41:29,430
So on the left, we have this
math word question benchmark

895
00:41:29,430 --> 00:41:30,360
called GSMAK.

896
00:41:30,360 --> 00:41:32,430
It's basically
questions that you'd

897
00:41:32,430 --> 00:41:35,250
see in an elementary
school math test.

898
00:41:35,250 --> 00:41:38,160
And you can see, the
blue dot is standard,

899
00:41:38,160 --> 00:41:41,115
and the purple star
is chain of thought.

900
00:41:41,115 --> 00:41:42,990
Basically, you see that
the chain of thought,

901
00:41:42,990 --> 00:41:46,200
if you use a large enough
model, does a lot better

902
00:41:46,200 --> 00:41:48,758
than standard prompting.

903
00:41:48,758 --> 00:41:50,550
And actually, it beats
the fine-tuned state

904
00:41:50,550 --> 00:41:53,640
of the art at the time.

905
00:41:53,640 --> 00:41:58,110
A similar example is on this
benchmark called StrategyQA.

906
00:41:58,110 --> 00:42:00,240
And what StrategyQA
is it's basically

907
00:42:00,240 --> 00:42:03,390
like this world knowledge
plus common-sense reasoning

908
00:42:03,390 --> 00:42:04,000
benchmark.

909
00:42:04,000 --> 00:42:05,760
So the question would
be like, can you

910
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:08,410
hide a basketball in
the sand cats ear?

911
00:42:08,410 --> 00:42:12,180
And then the model would say, a
basketball is about this size,

912
00:42:12,180 --> 00:42:14,982
a sand cat's ear is that,
so it would not fit.

913
00:42:14,982 --> 00:42:16,440
And on this benchmark,
you can also

914
00:42:16,440 --> 00:42:19,350
see that we can beat the
fine-tuned state of the art

915
00:42:19,350 --> 00:42:22,242
from before, just by
using chain of thought

916
00:42:22,242 --> 00:42:23,700
with a large enough
language model.

917
00:42:23,700 --> 00:42:28,090

918
00:42:28,090 --> 00:42:31,740
So one way we use this is that
we evaluate a chain of thought

919
00:42:31,740 --> 00:42:35,560
on a certain subset
of BIG-bench tasks.

920
00:42:35,560 --> 00:42:38,700
So we created the subset
called BIG-bench hard.

921
00:42:38,700 --> 00:42:41,760
And basically, it's like
23 challenging tasks

922
00:42:41,760 --> 00:42:45,060
from BIG-bench, where
no model had done better

923
00:42:45,060 --> 00:42:48,600
than the average human writer.

924
00:42:48,600 --> 00:42:50,700
So the way that you
prompt the model

925
00:42:50,700 --> 00:42:54,120
is that you'd have a task
description, question, options,

926
00:42:54,120 --> 00:42:55,740
chain of thought.

927
00:42:55,740 --> 00:42:58,990
And then the test
[AUDIO OUT] question.

928
00:42:58,990 --> 00:43:02,800
And so I'll give a couple
of examples of tasks here.

929
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:06,640
So one example is navigate.

930
00:43:06,640 --> 00:43:08,170
Basically, what
the language model

931
00:43:08,170 --> 00:43:11,660
has to do in this task is it
has to basically follow these--

932
00:43:11,660 --> 00:43:14,560
so the question is, if you
follow these instructions,

933
00:43:14,560 --> 00:43:16,157
do you return to
the starting point?

934
00:43:16,157 --> 00:43:18,490
Turn left, turn right, take
five steps, take four steps,

935
00:43:18,490 --> 00:43:21,040
turn around and take nine steps.

936
00:43:21,040 --> 00:43:25,510
And then, the model following
the [INAUDIBLE] exemplars

937
00:43:25,510 --> 00:43:30,250
is able to basically track
state after all of the actions.

938
00:43:30,250 --> 00:43:34,910
And then at the end it says,
OK, are we at the final answer--

939
00:43:34,910 --> 00:43:36,457
are we at the original location?

940
00:43:36,457 --> 00:43:38,290
If it is zero, zero,
then the answer is yes.

941
00:43:38,290 --> 00:43:41,340

942
00:43:41,340 --> 00:43:44,720
Just to give an example
of another task.

943
00:43:44,720 --> 00:43:47,180
Here's a task that's
very easy for humans,

944
00:43:47,180 --> 00:43:48,170
basically word sorting.

945
00:43:48,170 --> 00:43:50,420
So there's a list of words.

946
00:43:50,420 --> 00:43:52,650
Early, bela, I'm not
going to read them.

947
00:43:52,650 --> 00:43:55,520
And basically, the
model has to sort them

948
00:43:55,520 --> 00:43:57,967
in alphabetical order.

949
00:43:57,967 --> 00:44:00,300
And here, the model can follow
the [INAUDIBLE] exemplar.

950
00:44:00,300 --> 00:44:05,270
So you have this pretty
complicated chain of thought,

951
00:44:05,270 --> 00:44:08,900
where the model has to
sort each of the subparts,

952
00:44:08,900 --> 00:44:11,870
and then finally, it gets to the
final answer, which is correct.

953
00:44:11,870 --> 00:44:15,480

954
00:44:15,480 --> 00:44:20,030
So here's this result summary
on this subset of BIG-bench.

955
00:44:20,030 --> 00:44:24,420
So you can see, OK,
we have two metrics.

956
00:44:24,420 --> 00:44:27,530
One is just the average
performance on all these tasks,

957
00:44:27,530 --> 00:44:32,330
and the second is
percent of tasks that are

958
00:44:32,330 --> 00:44:34,820
above the average human writer.

959
00:44:34,820 --> 00:44:40,160
So average human writer is
67, max human writer is 94.

960
00:44:40,160 --> 00:44:43,040
And then prior results, the
model was doing way worse.

961
00:44:43,040 --> 00:44:44,540
It was like 50.

962
00:44:44,540 --> 00:44:49,850
And this is sort of by
construction of the subset.

963
00:44:49,850 --> 00:44:52,580
And then we use
DaVinci 0.2, which is

964
00:44:52,580 --> 00:44:54,470
like one of the open AI models.

965
00:44:54,470 --> 00:44:58,190
And actually you can use this
one for free with OpenAI API.

966
00:44:58,190 --> 00:45:01,370
And basically, if you
do answer only prompting

967
00:45:01,370 --> 00:45:03,560
without chain of
thought, then you sort of

968
00:45:03,560 --> 00:45:06,710
are being the average
human writer on 5 of 27.

969
00:45:06,710 --> 00:45:09,470
But if you use
chain-of-thought prompting,

970
00:45:09,470 --> 00:45:12,530
then the performance increases
by this pretty decent amount,

971
00:45:12,530 --> 00:45:15,560
and you're able to pass
the average human--

972
00:45:15,560 --> 00:45:18,110
on the majority of tasks.

973
00:45:18,110 --> 00:45:20,000
And then below is just
this visualization

974
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:23,210
of the tasks that are doing
worse than humans, in red,

975
00:45:23,210 --> 00:45:25,430
and then better
than humans in blue.

976
00:45:25,430 --> 00:45:26,510
Yeah?

977
00:45:26,510 --> 00:45:27,110
Two questions.

978
00:45:27,110 --> 00:45:29,510
One is, isn't this
similar to [INAUDIBLE],,

979
00:45:29,510 --> 00:45:32,450
in spirit at least?

980
00:45:32,450 --> 00:45:34,347
Is what similar?

981
00:45:34,347 --> 00:45:36,930
I think chain-of-thought prompt,
I'm not sure what [INAUDIBLE]

982
00:45:36,930 --> 00:45:41,050
in chain of thought.

983
00:45:41,050 --> 00:45:44,440
Yeah, I think it's--

984
00:45:44,440 --> 00:45:45,800
I wouldn't call it similar.

985
00:45:45,800 --> 00:45:47,788
So train of thought
is basically,

986
00:45:47,788 --> 00:45:49,330
you take a pretrained
language model,

987
00:45:49,330 --> 00:45:51,790
and you use a prompting
technique that includes

988
00:45:51,790 --> 00:45:54,310
intermediate reasoning path.

989
00:45:54,310 --> 00:45:58,180
The way that RLHF works is that
you have this additional data

990
00:45:58,180 --> 00:45:59,980
that you want to fine
tune the model on,

991
00:45:59,980 --> 00:46:02,440
and you have a
preference model that

992
00:46:02,440 --> 00:46:05,455
predicts how well does
a certain output--

993
00:46:05,455 --> 00:46:09,660

994
00:46:09,660 --> 00:46:12,250
how likely is that to
be preferred by humans?

995
00:46:12,250 --> 00:46:17,320
And then RLHF, what that
does is it fine tunes

996
00:46:17,320 --> 00:46:20,260
the language model to do
well on the preference models

997
00:46:20,260 --> 00:46:20,810
prediction.

998
00:46:20,810 --> 00:46:24,130
So basically, it's sort
of aligning the model

999
00:46:24,130 --> 00:46:26,215
with what humans would prefer.

1000
00:46:26,215 --> 00:46:27,340
Is there a second question?

1001
00:46:27,340 --> 00:46:29,840
Yeah, I'm sorry, it's here.

1002
00:46:29,840 --> 00:46:33,110
Grace asks, can chain of thought
be encoded in fine tuning,

1003
00:46:33,110 --> 00:46:36,570
rather than having to
be in the [INAUDIBLE]??

1004
00:46:36,570 --> 00:46:37,430
Yes.

1005
00:46:37,430 --> 00:46:39,873
The short answer is yes.

1006
00:46:39,873 --> 00:46:41,540
The sort of complicated
thing about that

1007
00:46:41,540 --> 00:46:44,990
is that you have to have
chain-of-thought intermediate

1008
00:46:44,990 --> 00:46:45,530
steps.

1009
00:46:45,530 --> 00:46:48,380
And those are pretty--

1010
00:46:48,380 --> 00:46:54,570
it can be costly to gather that
data and to do the fine tuning.

1011
00:46:54,570 --> 00:46:55,470
One last question.

1012
00:46:55,470 --> 00:46:56,783
Sorry for everybody.

1013
00:46:56,783 --> 00:46:58,200
Another student
asks, do you think

1014
00:46:58,200 --> 00:47:00,960
that chain-of-thought and
prompt engineering in general

1015
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:02,790
is just an artifact
that won't be

1016
00:47:02,790 --> 00:47:05,280
necessary for
